AU  - Kamping, Sandra; Flor, Herta
TI  - Brain Imaging of Muscle Pain
PY  - 2010
AB  - In this chapter, we review research on the central correlates of acute pain, discuss differences in the representation of cutaneous and muscle pain, specifics of cerebral correlates of acute pain applied in patients with musculoskeletal disorders, and variations in brain activity related to chronic muscle pain. In general, muscle pain seems to have a more affective connotation than cutaneous pain and thus is more disruptive, has more limbic involvement, and is also more prone to emotional learning and cognitive disturbances. Future research should examine in more detail the specifics of learning and memory processes in chronic muscle pain, and elucidate the role of affective and cognitive processing. More detailed knowledge about the cerebral processing of muscle pain would also open the way to new treatment options that focus more on central than peripheral mechanisms.
