AU  - Franklyn, Kate Louise; Guymer, Emma Kathryn; Littlejohn, Geoffrey O.
TI  - Targeting fibromyalgia pain: brain–spinal cord and peripheral contributions
PY  - 2011
AB  - Fibromyalgia (FM) is a complex chronic pain syndrome with a well-defined clinical phenotype that results from disordered central pain-related mechanisms. The etiology is multifaceted with links to genetic factors, personality, psychological distress, environmental triggers and peripheral musculoskeletal function. Central brain and spinal cord ‘top-down’ mechanisms dominate the pathophysiology of FM. We review the disordered pain-related neurological processes that are present in FM and discuss treatments that target these relevant mechanisms.
