AU  - Allen, Richard P.
TI  - Evaluation and Management of RLS and PLMD
PY  - 2017
AB  - The commonly occurring (5-7 % of adults) restless legs syndrome (RLS) has a wide range of severity. When severe it causes major life and health problems. Both genetic allelic variations and iron-compromising environmental factors increase the risk of RLS. RLS can be treated fairly well using α2δ ligands, dopamine agonists and iron. Longer term treatment with dopamine agonists may augment the RLS condition producing a profoundly severe RLS and should be used with caution in lower doses not exceeding regulatory approved dosing. The primary motor sign of RLS are periodic limb movements in sleep (PLMS). When PLMS occur without RLS and are associated with sleep or wake disturbances not otherwise explained, they signify the Periodic Limb Movement Disorder (PLMD). Since PLMS are a motor sign of RLS this chapter starts with describing the advances in PLMS and accordingly PLMD and then presents RLS.
